  "query_preview": "..Currently, a mid-level trough is traversing across the \nGreat Basin with its axis nearly centered along the UT/NV border. \nWith this trough, a cold front continues to sweep across much of UT, \nhaving already traversed the western half of the state. While the \nlower levels are slowly drying out, weak ascent with modest moisture \naloft will result in light to occasionally moderate rainfall \npersisting across much of the forecast area through roughly 09z. \nFrom 09z on, higher terrain will be favored for precipitation given \ndrier low levels of the atmosphere. As such, the flood watch will be \nallowed to expire at 06z for southern UT and portions of central UT. \n\n700mb temperatures will continue to cool following the passage of \nthe cold front to roughly -3C to -4C resulting in snow levels \ncontinuing to drop through the evening to around 6500ft. \nAdditionally, northwest flow aloft will persist, resulting in a \nfavorable pattern for widespread mountain snow across much of Utah's \nhigh terrain. Of note, a TROWAL feature has been identified via \nsatellite imagery serving to wrap more moisture around the northwest \nside of a deepening upper low located over northern UT. This has \nresulted in another round of widespread light to moderate rain \nobserved across northern UT, and will also contribute to snowfall \ntotals across our northern UT mountains. Across our UT high terrain \n(generally 9000ft or greater), 3-6\" of additional snowfall is \nexpected. Some areas, such as the Upper Cottonwoods and Western \nUintas may see additional snowfall totals from 6-12\" given favorable \nnorthwest flow orientation overnight. \n\nTransient ridging builds in Monday in the wake of the deepening \ntrough as it progresses east. This will result in most precipitation \ncoming to a halt across the forecast area, though some lingering \nshowers across the UT high terrain may persist. This feature is \nforecast to persist through Tuesday bringing quiescent conditions \nand unseasonably cool temperatures, potentially as low a 15F below \nseasonal normals areawide. \n\nEnsemble guidance has come into better agreement with a weak mid-\nlevel trough sweeping through the Great Basin on Wednesday. This \nsystem is not expected to bring a whole lot of moisture with it, and \nwith weak forcing, only light valley rainfall with light mountain \nsnow accumulations are expected across northern and central UT. \nFollowing this system, dry conditions develop once again as ridging \nbecomes dominant across much of the western U.S. through the \nremainder of the week and into the weekend.",
